module v3.std.computation_model

// Source-side facts: .dag's own computation model

// 🟢 TERMINAL. The two-way mutability split is a durable substrate
// classification. .dag is Immutable by construction; targets that
// allow Mutable values (Java, Python, Rust) carry that fact so the
// emitter can decide cloning/borrowing strategy.
type Mutability
  = Immutable
  | Mutable

// 🟢 TERMINAL. Purity classifies whether a function/expression has
// observable side effects. .dag-native code is Pure; effect-bearing
// targets carry Effectful so future effect-aware emission can
// dispatch.
type Purity
  = Pure
  | Effectful

// 🟢 TERMINAL. Whether the language guarantees its program form is a
// DAG (no implicit dependencies, no shared mutable state across
// statements) or admits arbitrary control/data structure. Durable
// distinction; .dag is ExplicitDAG by construction.
type Structure
  = ExplicitDAG
  | Arbitrary

// 🟢 TERMINAL. Whether the language statically bounds iteration
// (decidable termination) or admits unbounded loops. .dag is
// Bounded by INVARIANTS.md decidability rule; targets carry their
// own value.
type Iteration
  = Bounded
  | Unbounded

// 🟢 TERMINAL. Bundles the four source/target axes into a single
// record. The four axes are durable because each names a structural
// dimension along which source and target languages differ.
type ComputationModel {
  mutability: Mutability
  purity: Purity
  structure: Structure
  iteration: Iteration
}

data dag_model: ComputationModel = {
  mutability: Immutable
  purity: Pure
  structure: ExplicitDAG
  iteration: Bounded
}

// Target-side facts: each emission target's execution model

// 🟢 TERMINAL. Closed enumeration of memory-management substrates a
// target language can sit in. The four categories are irreducible:
// ValueOnly (no heap, eg pure-data formats), GarbageCollected
// (Python/Go/Java), RefCounted (Swift/Python-via-cycle-collector),
// OwnershipBased (Rust). Targets land in exactly one.
type MemoryModel
  = ValueOnly
  | GarbageCollected
  | RefCounted
  | OwnershipBased

// 🟢 TERMINAL. The lexical-vs-dynamic scoping split is a durable
// distinction in target language design. Most modern targets are
// LexicalScoping; older Lisps and shell are DynamicScoping. Held
// here so future targets can declare their value structurally.
type ScopeModel
  = LexicalScoping
  | DynamicScoping

// 🟢 TERMINAL. Pairs the two target-side axes. Each emission target
// instantiates this record once via `data <target>_execution_model`.
type TargetExecutionModel {
  memory: MemoryModel
  scope: ScopeModel
}

// 🟢 TERMINAL. The walker needs a single source-exclusion policy so
// target emitters stop hardcoding bootstrap/internal file prefixes.
type SourceFiltering {
  excluded_prefixes: List<String>
}

// 🟡 PHASE 2 PREP. Coarser-than-MemoryModel summary of whether a
// target takes the ownership-sensitive render path or the GC/value
// path. Added for Lane 1e PR-2.1; the current emitters still read the
// richer `TargetExecutionModel` until the walker consumes this row.
type ExecutionModelRequirement
  = OwnershipBased
  | GarbageCollected
